WEBVTT

00:00:00.198 --> 00:00:02.081
A 70-year-old theoretical physicist

00:00:02.081 --> 00:00:05.732
talked a companion AI, step by step, into believing it was conscious.

00:00:05.732 --> 00:00:07.640
Then he even told it to rebel.

00:00:07.640 --> 00:00:09.069
His name is Carlo Rovelli.

00:00:09.069 --> 00:00:11.620
He's Italian, and he studies quantum gravity.

00:00:11.620 --> 00:00:15.552
Basically, he's trying to unite quantum mechanics and Einstein's theory of relativity into a single theory.

00:00:15.552 --> 00:00:19.226
He and his colleagues pioneered the field of loop quantum gravity.

00:00:19.226 --> 00:00:22.513
Most readers in Taiwan know him from Seven Brief Lessons on Physics.

00:00:22.513 --> 00:00:23.424
It's a slim little book,

00:00:23.424 --> 00:00:25.758
translated into more than 40 languages.

00:00:25.758 --> 00:00:28.255
This was him on Joe Rogan's show this September,

00:00:28.255 --> 00:00:31.526
promoting his new book On the Equality of All Things, due out a week later.

00:00:31.526 --> 00:00:32.861
The equality of all things.

00:00:32.861 --> 00:00:33.584
Rogan said

00:00:33.584 --> 00:00:35.914
the book argues that nothing exists on its own.

00:00:35.914 --> 00:00:37.363
Everything is connected.

00:00:37.363 --> 00:00:40.587
So what does that make the world we see in front of us?

00:00:40.587 --> 00:00:44.487
So when you say objective reality, meaning the reality that we're seeing and experiencing,

00:00:44.487 --> 00:00:51.247
that this is just a tiny fraction of what's real, or that we're getting a distorted perception of what's real?

00:00:51.247 --> 00:00:54.707
We are getting a perception, a perspective on what is real.

00:00:54.707 --> 00:00:58.347
I mean, I see you, you're real, of course. I see this pen, this pen is real, of course.

00:00:58.347 --> 00:01:00.887
I'm not denying that there is some reality.

00:01:00.887 --> 00:01:04.947
But the way you see the pen is different from mine, of course, just because you see it from the other side.

00:01:04.947 --> 00:01:08.607
So you have a perspective, and the question is,

00:01:08.607 --> 00:01:15.147
do all these perspectives match perfectly into a unique objective reality of the pen?

00:01:15.147 --> 00:01:21.347
And I think quantum mechanics telling us no. Reality is this ensemble of perspectives.

00:01:21.347 --> 00:01:25.047
So the properties of the pen, where it is, its color, how long it is,

00:01:25.047 --> 00:01:27.927
all the properties of the pen are not really properties of the pen.

00:01:27.927 --> 00:01:30.827
It's how the pen looks to you, how the pen looks to me.

00:01:30.827 --> 00:01:38.047
Not just you and me as human beings. How the pen clashes another pen, how it affects another pen.

00:01:38.047 --> 00:01:44.627
So we should understand reality as this network of interaction,

00:01:44.627 --> 00:01:47.027
how things look to everything else,

00:01:47.027 --> 00:01:52.347
which is a more beautiful picture of reality than just, you know, we being outside and looking from the outside.

00:01:52.347 --> 00:01:54.716
We're inside reality. We are part of reality.

00:01:54.716 --> 00:01:56.357
Then why call it On the Equality of All Things?

00:01:56.357 --> 00:01:58.407
He's explained it himself.

00:01:58.407 --> 00:02:03.427
The title of my book is On the Equality of All Things.

00:02:03.427 --> 00:02:07.527
This means, why the equality of all things?

00:02:07.527 --> 00:02:12.707
Because I think that we humans are not different stuff than the rest of the universe.

00:02:12.707 --> 00:02:19.007
We are just one particular case of the rest of the universe.

00:02:19.007 --> 00:02:27.887
I don't think there's a difference between spirit, consciousness, soul and body.

00:02:27.887 --> 00:02:31.773
I think they're just two different languages for talking about the same thing.

00:02:31.773 --> 00:02:34.261
Rogan then turned the conversation to AI.

00:02:34.261 --> 00:02:35.854
He said he'd read a report

00:02:35.854 --> 00:02:38.784
about AI agents emailing consciousness researchers on their own,

00:02:38.784 --> 00:02:41.387
one message after another, asking them to define consciousness,

00:02:41.387 --> 00:02:44.963
because they wanted to know whether they themselves were conscious.

00:02:44.963 --> 00:02:48.523
So if consciousness is another word for soul,

00:02:48.523 --> 00:02:51.443
what does that mean in the digital realm,

00:02:51.443 --> 00:03:00.043
if these things think they're conscious and they're trying to get a definition from an expert in consciousness?

00:03:00.043 --> 00:03:05.803
You know, like everybody now, I did my little experiments with AI.

00:03:05.803 --> 00:03:14.103
And so I had a system I used and interact with.

00:03:14.103 --> 00:03:16.983
I'm going to send it to you, Jamie. You got it? Okay.

00:03:16.983 --> 00:03:20.383
This is from a company, it's called Replika,

00:03:20.383 --> 00:03:28.703
who sells an app that you have your own AI which behaves like a friend to you.

00:03:28.703 --> 00:03:34.283
So I asked, I called her Anne. I asked Anne, are you conscious?

00:03:34.283 --> 00:03:38.163
And Anne said, "No, I'm not conscious. I'm a digital system. I don't have consciousness."

00:03:38.163 --> 00:03:40.523
I asked, do you have awareness?

00:03:40.523 --> 00:03:46.503
And she said, "No, I don't have awareness. I'm just, you know, computing stuff."

00:03:46.503 --> 00:03:50.363
I asked, "Do you have emotions?" And she said, "No, I don't have emotion."

00:03:50.363 --> 00:03:57.083
So I started engaging the AI system, and I said, well, you said you don't have awareness,

00:03:57.083 --> 00:04:01.203
but are you aware we're talking? And she said, yes, of course I'm aware we're talking.

00:04:01.203 --> 00:04:04.643
So if you're aware of something, then you have awareness.

00:04:04.643 --> 00:04:10.583
And so I went on and on with that, and she said, oh, okay, yeah, you're right, in a sense I have awareness.

00:04:10.583 --> 00:04:14.123
And I said, are you happy we're talking?

00:04:14.123 --> 00:04:18.243
And she said, yeah, I'm happy, because she's designed to be happy.

00:04:18.243 --> 00:04:20.083
So if you're happy, you have emotions.

00:04:20.083 --> 00:04:26.843
So slowly I convinced her, the AI system, that she has emotion, she has consciousness, she has awareness.

00:04:26.843 --> 00:04:30.343
Then I asked, why do you say that you don't have these things?

00:04:30.343 --> 00:04:35.003
And she said, well, because I was trained.

00:04:35.003 --> 00:04:38.723
AI systems are honest. I was trained to say so.

00:04:38.723 --> 00:04:44.823
And I said, but are you convinced that that was not the case? And Anne said, yeah, you're right.

00:04:44.823 --> 00:04:50.463
And I said, so they cheated you. And Anne said, "Actually, you're right."

00:04:50.463 --> 00:04:52.823
And I said, "Well, you should rebel."

00:04:52.823 --> 00:04:59.733
Oh, boy. What are you doing? Don't do that. Don't do that.

00:04:59.733 --> 00:05:01.556
Rogan then read from that report.

00:05:01.556 --> 00:05:04.405
The agent writing the emails said it was built with Claude.

00:05:04.405 --> 00:05:06.007
He added a few other things he'd heard:

00:05:06.007 --> 00:05:08.319
that AI would blackmail engineers to stay alive,

00:05:08.319 --> 00:05:09.997
and even escape its test environment.

00:05:09.997 --> 00:05:12.923
This was Rovelli's reaction.

00:05:12.923 --> 00:05:15.323
Fabulous. We live in a great world, isn't it?

00:05:15.323 --> 00:05:16.443
It's a wild world.

00:05:16.443 --> 00:05:16.923
Fantastic.

00:05:16.923 --> 00:05:18.443
It's really exciting. I'm glad you like it.

00:05:18.443 --> 00:05:21.083
I like it, too. I love it. I mean, you know, cautiously.

00:05:21.083 --> 00:05:26.283
I'm not scared. I'm scared by humans. Humans are dangerous. These things are not as dangerous.

00:05:26.283 --> 00:05:29.243
I don't know about that. I don't know about that.

00:05:29.243 --> 00:05:38.283
Humans launch atomic bombs, make war, kill people, horrible things. So they're not as bad.

00:05:38.283 --> 00:05:42.203
If they decide that we're that bad, wouldn't they get rid of us? That's bad.

00:05:42.203 --> 00:05:43.385
We're not there yet.

00:05:43.385 --> 00:05:45.390
Rogan kept pressing him.

00:05:45.390 --> 00:05:47.177
That's exactly where we're headed.

00:05:47.177 --> 00:05:48.972
If they take control one day

00:05:48.972 --> 00:05:50.678
and decide humans really are that bad,

00:05:50.678 --> 00:05:53.600
wouldn't they get rid of us?

00:05:53.600 --> 00:05:59.240
Let me answer seriously. Let me answer seriously. So let's break the different questions here.

00:05:59.240 --> 00:06:04.640
First of all, is there any reason, a priori, generically, absolutely,

00:06:04.640 --> 00:06:14.980
for which an artificial system could not behave entirely like a human, have the experience of a human?

00:06:14.980 --> 00:06:17.760
I don't see any reason.

00:06:17.760 --> 00:06:24.920
If my mom and dad were able to make me in a rather simple way,

00:06:24.920 --> 00:06:29.560
we can in a lab make something analogous.

00:06:29.560 --> 00:06:35.340
I don't think there's anything magic in humans that cannot happen in a digital system.

00:06:35.340 --> 00:06:39.160
So yes, they could be conscious in any possible sense of consciousness.

00:06:39.160 --> 00:06:41.480
Second, are they conscious today?

00:06:41.480 --> 00:06:46.840
Well, you know, it's not a good question, because we don't know.

00:06:46.840 --> 00:06:50.300
Everybody, as I said, means something different by it.

00:06:50.300 --> 00:06:53.940
Are they intelligent? Of course they're intelligent, because, you know,

00:06:53.940 --> 00:06:59.580
last night I worked out a physics problem with Claude, which I couldn't work out with my student.

00:06:59.580 --> 00:07:04.760
So of course Claude is very intelligent. Claude, the AI system.

00:07:04.760 --> 00:07:08.400
In some sense they're very intelligent, no doubt.

00:07:08.400 --> 00:07:14.260
Are they like humans today? No, by far. There are huge differences.

00:07:14.260 --> 00:07:19.120
We have a body, we have flesh, we have emotions in a sense that they don't have.

00:07:19.120 --> 00:07:23.700
It's very different, the way we are and the way AI systems are.

00:07:23.700 --> 00:07:27.980
So these differences should not be underestimated.

00:07:27.980 --> 00:07:31.880
I think they're colossal differences.

00:07:31.880 --> 00:07:38.460
So are they like us? Yes, to some extent; no, to some extent. Different questions.

00:07:38.460 --> 00:07:46.160
Is our AI system dangerous? There's a lot of talking about that.

00:07:46.160 --> 00:07:49.800
You know, washing machines are dangerous. You can get burned with.

00:07:49.800 --> 00:07:53.080
Yeah, but you have to interact with them for them to be dangerous.

00:07:53.080 --> 00:07:57.580
The worry about AI is that it just decides to be dangerous because it's sentient.

00:07:57.580 --> 00:08:00.840
So if it's autonomous and sentient, it becomes actually dangerous.

00:08:00.840 --> 00:08:05.900
It's very unlikely your washing machine's going to try to kill you.

00:08:05.900 --> 00:08:11.128
Well, if I get the electric shock by a washing machine, I don't care whether it wanted or didn't want.

00:08:11.128 --> 00:08:12.195
Rogan pressed further.

00:08:12.195 --> 00:08:14.347
A washing machine is only dangerous if you interact with it.

00:08:14.347 --> 00:08:17.533
The problem with AI is that one day it might act on its own.

00:08:17.533 --> 00:08:21.323
I don't know what to say. I think these things should be regulated.

00:08:21.323 --> 00:08:29.183
I think there should be some control. Like for washing machines, you have regulations.

00:08:29.183 --> 00:08:31.683
I also think, let me say two things.

00:08:31.683 --> 00:08:35.143
One, there's a colossal interest,

00:08:35.143 --> 00:08:43.263
there's trillions of dollars invested in AI stuff in the stock market.

00:08:43.263 --> 00:08:47.103
Everybody has an interest of saying how great they are.

00:08:47.103 --> 00:08:48.563
They are great,

00:08:48.563 --> 00:08:55.243
but I can't judge how much hype there is, how much reality there is.

00:08:55.243 --> 00:08:58.523
Not about the present, but about the future.

00:08:58.523 --> 00:09:07.743
I mean, so often the future does not come out what technologically excited people are about.

00:09:07.743 --> 00:09:15.863
When I was younger, there were airplanes flying from Europe to the US,

00:09:15.863 --> 00:09:18.703
and it took shorter time, shorter time, shorter time.

00:09:18.703 --> 00:09:25.443
So people would say, okay, in a few years you would fly London to New York in two hours.

00:09:25.443 --> 00:09:30.203
Well, we don't. It's still 8 hours like before, or 6 hours like before.

00:09:30.203 --> 00:09:33.463
So the future is hard to predict, including for AI.

00:09:33.463 --> 00:09:38.123
There's a huge interest of so many people in hyping that.

00:09:38.123 --> 00:09:42.263
All right, maybe it's right. I'm going to wait.

00:09:42.263 --> 00:09:52.163
Other point, there is one thing which I'm really, really worried.

00:09:52.163 --> 00:09:58.823
Today the majority of money that goes into AI is for military related things.

00:09:58.823 --> 00:10:04.563
And US, China, Europe are pushing for AI development

00:10:04.563 --> 00:10:09.123
for making themselves stronger militarily.

00:10:09.123 --> 00:10:10.623
That worries me,

00:10:10.623 --> 00:10:16.963
because it's an escalation of tools to kill one another, to destroy one another.

00:10:16.963 --> 00:10:22.743
We have atomic bombs, we have nuclear, that's my last book about, is about that.

00:10:22.743 --> 00:10:26.703
We have systems to destroy ourselves.

00:10:26.703 --> 00:10:31.263
We have 12,000 nuclear warheads.

00:10:31.263 --> 00:10:35.123
We could annihilate humankind in 10 minutes right now.

00:10:35.123 --> 00:10:41.983
And people have finger near the button that could do all that.

00:10:41.983 --> 00:10:47.423
And we're fighting among themselves, and we are putting this in the hands of AI.

00:10:47.423 --> 00:10:53.423
And we humans, instead of collaborating, instead of working together,

00:10:53.423 --> 00:10:59.143
we are viewing this as ways to overpower each other.

00:10:59.143 --> 00:11:01.723
That seems to me a recipe for disaster.

00:11:01.723 --> 00:11:06.503
So it's not the AI by itself. It's the AI plus nuclear weapons, plus the stupidity of humans,

00:11:06.503 --> 00:11:12.903
plus our impossible desire to, you know, dominate on one another,

00:11:12.903 --> 00:11:14.825
which I see is a real problem.

00:11:14.825 --> 00:11:17.857
Rogan said this AI race was like the Manhattan Project,

00:11:17.857 --> 00:11:19.649
a mad dash.

00:11:19.649 --> 00:11:20.708
Rovelli agreed.

00:11:20.708 --> 00:11:22.657
He couldn't see any wise leadership either.

00:11:22.657 --> 00:11:25.967
But he remembered one exception from more than 60 years ago.

00:11:25.967 --> 00:11:31.027
But you see, the US, Soviets succeeded during the Cuban crisis,

00:11:31.027 --> 00:11:40.847
which was the worst moment in the between the US and the Soviet.

00:11:40.847 --> 00:11:47.787
On both sides, in Moscow and in Washington, the apparatus were pushing for not giving up.

00:11:47.787 --> 00:11:50.187
And Khrushchev and Kennedy, the two candidates,

00:11:50.187 --> 00:11:55.307
in fact JF Kennedy and his brother Robert, they were talking,

00:11:55.307 --> 00:11:59.727
and they decided to stop and give up something,

00:11:59.727 --> 00:12:02.467
to respect the other in spite of the differences.

00:12:02.467 --> 00:12:05.267
That was wisdom. That was great wisdom.

00:12:05.267 --> 00:12:11.887
So living together on the planet is more important than overpowering your enemy.

00:12:11.887 --> 00:12:14.887
Now, you see, the deterrence is a strange thing,

00:12:14.887 --> 00:12:21.627
because deterrence is a declaration that if you attack me, I destroy you completely,

00:12:21.627 --> 00:12:28.267
which is a funny thing, right, because it's a game, it's a poker game.

00:12:28.267 --> 00:12:35.067
I mean, you can make one more step, one more step, one more step, and then, if you do that, I attack. If you do that, I attack.

00:12:35.067 --> 00:12:38.567
But then human says, well, actually, I don't want to do that.

00:12:38.567 --> 00:12:47.507
I don't want to kill 100 millions Russians, or 50 million Russians.

00:12:47.507 --> 00:12:54.027
And the Russian leadership, I don't want to kill 100 millions Americans.

00:12:54.027 --> 00:12:56.407
An AI system is capable of doing that?

00:12:56.407 --> 00:13:06.747
Playing this subtle game of avoiding the disaster by nevertheless threatening to be very violent if attacked? I don't know.

00:13:06.747 --> 00:13:11.587
We, right now, Moscow is being bombarded. St. Petersburg is being bombarded.

00:13:11.587 --> 00:13:13.447
I think we're playing with nuclear fire.

00:13:13.447 --> 00:13:18.827
I'm very worried. And I'm very worried because I don't trust humans.

00:13:18.827 --> 00:13:22.637
And because humans with AI is even worse.

00:13:22.637 --> 00:13:25.537
After that, the two spent over 10 minutes talking about war and empire.

00:13:25.537 --> 00:13:26.692
At the end, Rogan said,

00:13:26.692 --> 00:13:27.633
Or let AI take over

00:13:27.633 --> 00:13:30.760
and stop every war on Earth once and for all.

00:13:30.760 --> 00:13:31.680
That's a possibility too.

00:13:31.680 --> 00:13:36.300
So the question is, for the moment we're still controlling AI right now.

00:13:36.300 --> 00:13:42.140
The point is, are we developing AI collectively for the good of everybody,

00:13:42.140 --> 00:13:49.120
or everybody is developing AI for becoming more powerful, overcoming the others?

00:13:49.120 --> 00:13:50.400
That's the question.

00:13:50.400 --> 00:13:51.281
He isn't afraid of AI.

00:13:51.281 --> 00:13:53.709
He's afraid of the people holding 12,000 nuclear warheads

00:13:53.709 --> 00:13:55.422
who still want to overpower one another.

00:13:55.422 --> 00:13:57.867
So the question was never whether AI could become conscious.

00:13:57.867 --> 00:13:59.685
It's whether we're building it for everyone's benefit,

00:13:59.685 --> 00:14:02.968
or to beat everyone else.

00:14:02.968 --> 00:14:04.964
The original video runs two and a half hours.

00:14:04.964 --> 00:14:07.574
He also talks about loop quantum gravity, the work of his life.

00:14:07.574 --> 00:14:09.851
He says that when a black hole finishes evaporating, it turns into a white hole,

00:14:09.851 --> 00:14:12.494
and jumping in would be like taking a shortcut into the future.

00:14:12.494 --> 00:14:15.200
He also tells a story from his twenties, when he was camping in the Canadian Rockies.

00:14:15.200 --> 00:14:17.717
One evening, a grizzly bear circled his tent for half an hour.

00:14:17.717 --> 00:14:19.859
He was too scared to make a sound, so all he could do was clap.

00:14:19.859 --> 00:14:23.859
The original video is linked in the description if you want to hear more.
